cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "Cite the software version used and the associated RCC paper."
title: "RCC RefCert: A Research Toolkit for Structure-Fair Quantum Circuit Complexity"
type: software
version: 0.4.0
url: "https://github.com/yeruciwenrou-dotcom/rcc-refcert"
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/yeruciwenrou-dotcom/rcc-refcert"
abstract: >-
  RCC RefCert is an open-source toolkit for studying quantum circuit
  complexity. Given a preparation model and final-state data, it computes a
  rigorous lower bound on optimal quantum circuit complexity. The inputs can
  be an exact target spectrum or finite measurement data collected under a
  prespecified protocol. The toolkit implements the Reference-Contingent
  Complexity (RCC) framework of Liu, Tian and Wu.
  Structural fairness takes the structure each background reliably supplies
  as its reference zero point. Controls and costs are fixed before target
  selection. RCC counts only the resources used to generate additional
  structure and measures them on a common calibrated scale, making quantum
  circuit complexity comparable across physical settings.
  A terminal-state certificate
  bounds the cost of every circuit that prepares the target to the stated
  accuracy. Researchers can build finite-control quantum models, verify
  reference certificates and compare candidate preparations. In exact
  diagonal-preserving models, the lower bound and the cost of a successful
  program bracket the optimum. Their difference bounds the possible saving,
  and equality proves the program optimal. General matrix models provide
  numerical candidate diagnostics. Window RCC resolves how
  complexity is distributed across the subsystems of pure and mixed states
  at a common reference and control bandwidth, including the local and
  correlation contributions along a preparation. For a prespecified binary
  measurement, researchers can plan a sample size for a preparation-cost
  threshold and certify a lower bound from target and reference counts.
  Complete outcome histograms give confidence bounds on the RCC difference
  between two states in the same classical measurement window. A shared analysis
  bundle carries the inputs and results for independent recomputation.
keywords:
  - quantum circuit complexity
  - structure-fairness
  - reference-contingent complexity
  - quantum information
  - quantum computing
  - quantum state preparation
  - quantum channels
  - reference certificates
  - algorithmic information theory
  - quantum complexity
  - theoretical physics
  - high-energy physics
